provenance.steps_response:
[
  {
    "description": "With a fresh start and no explicit port configuration applied, inspect the administrative negotiation setting on ports that support auto-negotiation across the available media types. Confirm auto-negotiation is the default state and that the reported port state distinguishes the configured setting from the operational result.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "Connect the DUT to a link partner on a native copper port with both ends set to auto-negotiation, using straight-through cabling. Confirm the link comes up, that the negotiated speed and duplex match on both ends, and that the resolved MDI/MDIX polarity is reported.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "Repeat the copper auto/auto link with crossed cabling in place of straight-through. Confirm polarity is resolved automatically and the same negotiated speed and duplex result is reached as with straight cabling.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "Establish an auto/auto link on a fibre port using a supported transceiver, selecting transceiver types from the list agreed with SVT so that more than one supported speed grade is exercised. Confirm the link establishes and the negotiated values are consistent with the inserted transceiver's capabilities.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "Establish an auto/auto link on a copper SFP transceiver, exercising both straight-through and crossed cabling. Confirm the link establishes, polarity resolves automatically, and the negotiated result matches that obtained on the equivalent native copper port.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "Restrict the capabilities advertised by the link partner so that the highest common capability is below the maximum either port can support, covering a lower speed and a half-duplex case where the platform supports it. Confirm the negotiated speed and duplex settle on the highest capability common to both ends rather than the port maximum.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "Pass bidirectional traffic across an established auto/auto link at the negotiated speed and duplex, using frame sizes spanning the supported range and offered load up to line rate for the negotiated speed. Confirm traffic is forwarded in both directions without loss and without error counter growth on either end.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "On an established auto/auto link, force re-negotiation by each of the available means in turn — removing and restoring the cable, administratively disabling and re-enabling the port, and restarting the device. Confirm the link re-establishes in each case and the negotiated speed, duplex and polarity return to the same values as before.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "While an auto/auto link is up, change the capabilities advertised by the link partner to a different speed or duplex grade without touching the DUT configuration. Confirm the DUT updates its negotiated speed and duplex to the new highest common capability with no manual intervention on the DUT.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "Leave the DUT port on auto-negotiation while fixing the link partner to a specific speed and duplex, covering both a matching-speed and a differing-speed partner setting. Confirm the resulting link state and duplex behaviour follow the platform's documented fallback rules and that the reported operational values reflect what was actually resolved.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "Configure the two ends so that they share no common speed or duplex capability. Confirm no link is brought up, or that a fault state is clearly reported, and that the port does not flap between states while the mismatch persists.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "Attempt to enable auto-negotiation on a port or inserted transceiver type that does not support it. Confirm the request is rejected with a clear error and that the port's prior configuration and link state are preserved.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "With a fibre port in an auto/auto configuration, remove the transceiver and insert a different supported type with different capabilities, then return to the original. Confirm the port re-negotiates to values in line with each inserted transceiver and that removal and insertion are reported in the port state.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "Across the link-up, link-loss and re-negotiation events exercised above, review the logging, statistics, counters and management interfaces available on the platform. Confirm link state and negotiation transitions are recorded consistently and that the negotiated values are reported identically across the available management paths.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "Where the platform supports energy-efficient Ethernet or low-power idle, enable and disable it on an auto/auto link. Confirm negotiation still completes to the expected values and that the link remains stable with traffic running.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  },
  {
    "description": "Cycle the link repeatedly through negotiation — by cable or port disable/enable — over a sustained run on both copper and fibre ports. Confirm the negotiated result is consistent on every cycle, that no unexpected flapping or error counter growth occurs, and that device resource usage does not trend upward across the run.",
    "expectedResult": ""
  }
]

